## Key Points

| Concern | How it's handled |
|---|---|
| Mutual exclusion | Every access to `items`, `head`, `tail`, and `count` happens under `lock`. |
| Spurious wakeups | Waits use `while (condition) await();`, never `if`. |
| Lost wakeups | Signals are sent while holding the lock, right after the state change. |
| Unlock on exception | Every `lock` is paired with `unlock()` in a `finally` block. |
| Cancellation | `lockInterruptibly()` and `await()` both throw `InterruptedException`. |
| Memory leaks | Dequeued slots are set to `null`. |
| Nulls | They are rejected, so `null` can unambiguously mean "timed out" in `poll`. |

A possible extension is the two-lock design used by `LinkedBlockingQueue`. It uses separate put and take locks backed by an `AtomicInteger` count, so producers and consumers don't contend with each other. That raises throughput but makes the code more complex. The single-lock version above is the same approach `ArrayBlockingQueue` uses.
